7.16.m:
Whoever recites this slaying of Andhaka in an assembly of twice-born [brahmins], / if he is a brahmin, he obtains […] protection without doubt;
he finds […] bodily [health], yoga, […] beauty, and righteousness; / and having […] he is joined to the leadership of attendants upon the end of his body.
Thus ends the one hundred and fifty-sixth chapter in the Skandapurāṇa, in [the section on] the war of the attendants, on the proclamation of the boons to the gods.

In the word *brāhmaṇa*, the root *aṇ* denotes eating, in the sense of great oblation;
because he eats Brahman, he is called a *brāhmaṇa*.
Those who, born again, are invested with the sacred thread by high-souled men,
therefore, O goddess, the designation *dvija* (‘twice-born’) is ever applied to them.
*Viyat* is called the sky, and the letter *p* is remembered to mean bringing [there];
they cause one to reach heaven; therefore, O goddess, they are called *vipras*.
For these brahmins were created by the self-manifest [Brahmā] out of the essence of truth;
if they are displeased, success cannot be attained even by the gods.
